  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 544 PM MDT Thu May 14 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThis afternoon there is an upper-level trough over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This afternoon there remains a \nstrong pressure gradient. This combined with strong low and mid-\nlevel winds will bring windy conditions to North-central Montana.\nThis afternoon across the plains of North-central Montana the \nstrong winds are causing blowing dust to reduce visibility. This \nafternoon there will be snow showers along the Rocky Mountain \nFront. This afternoon temperatures will be around seasonal \naverages. On Friday zonal flow is over North-central, Central, and\nSouthwestern Montana. This will allow it to be mostly dry with \nabout seasonal averages for temperatures.\n\nOn Saturday an upper-level trough associated with a surface cold \nfront begins to move over North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana. This will bring precipitation and cooler temperatures to \nthe area on Saturday. On Sunday the upper-level trough remains over \nNorth-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This will bring \nwell-below seasonal averages for temperatures on Sunday. On Sunday \nthere will be widespread precipitation across the area. Across \nmountain locations this will be primarily snow. At lower-elevations \nthis will be a rain/snow mix. Sunday afternoon there is enough \ninstability around that there is the potential for a few rumbles of \nthunder as well. On Monday the upper-level trough remains over North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana. As a result \nprecipitation and cold temperatures (well-below) seasonal averages \nwill linger across the area. On Tuesday and Wednesday an upper-level \nridge builds in over North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana. This will allow temperatures to gradually warm up with \nmostly dry weather. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nOn Saturday there is a 10 - 20% chance for thunder across \nSouthwestern Montana. On Sunday there is a 15 - 25% chance for \nthunder across most of North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana. Sunday through Monday there is a 10 - 50% chance for \ngreater than a tenth of an inch of snow across the lower-elevations \nof North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana south of the \nMontana 200 Corridor. Across the mountains of North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana there is a 50 - 80% chance for 4 \ninches of snow or greater. One area of uncertainty with this \nsnowfall is when it falls will have drastic differences on impacts. \nIf snow falls during daylight hours there will be minimal impacts. \nHowever if snowfall falls over night this has the potential to lead \nto some impacts. Another area of uncertainty with this systems \nprecipitation types is when the cold air moves in and transitions \nprecipitation at the lower-eelvations from rain to snow. \n\nOn Sunday across most of the lower-elevations of North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana there is a 20 - 50% chance for \nhigh temperatures of 45 degrees or colder. Monday morning there is\na 30 - 50% chance for low temperatures of 28 degrees or colder \nacross the lower-elevations of North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. On Sunday morning across the lower-\nelevations of Southwestern Montana there is greater than a 40% \nchance for low temperatures of 28 degrees or colder. -IG",
